const e={minutes:"25–35",objectives:["Choose a greeting for the setting.","Ask how someone is and answer.","Use familiar tu/toi and polite vous consistently."],blocks:[{title:"Greeting versus goodbye",text:"Bonjour is a daytime hello. Bonsoir is an evening hello. Salut is informal and can mean hi or bye. Au revoir is a general goodbye. Bonne journée wishes someone a good day as you leave.",examples:[{fr:"Bonjour, madame.",en:"Hello, madam.",why:"A polite greeting."},{fr:"Salut, Lina !",en:"Hi, Lina!",why:"An informal greeting to someone you know."}]},{title:"Two ways to say you",text:"Tu addresses one person familiarly. Vous addresses one person politely or several people. Start with vous with an adult stranger. To return a question, say et toi ? for tu, or et vous ? for vous.",examples:[{fr:"Vous allez bien ?",en:"Are you well?",why:"Polite or plural."},{fr:"Ça va ? — Oui, ça va bien, merci. Et toi ?",en:"How are you? — Yes, I’m well, thank you. And you?",why:"A familiar exchange. Ça va is a fixed phrase."}]},{title:"Answer honestly in short phrases",text:"Très bien means very well; bien means well; pas mal means not bad; ça ne va pas means things are not going well. Merci is natural after answering.",examples:[{fr:"Pas mal, merci.",en:"Not bad, thank you.",why:"Pas mal is mildly positive."},{fr:"À demain !",en:"See you tomorrow!",why:"Use only if you expect to meet tomorrow."}]}],pronunciation:[["The sound ç makes","In ça, ç has an s sound. The final a is pronounced; do not say an English k sound."],["Connected phrases","Say au revoir and à bientôt as whole chunks. French r is made farther back in the mouth than most English r sounds."]],worked:[{prompt:"Return a polite “Vous allez bien ?” question.",steps:["Vous signals a polite or plural interaction.","Answer how you are and say merci.","Return the question using vous, not toi."],answer:"Très bien, merci. Et vous ?",english:"Very well, thank you. And you?"}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"You enter a shop in the morning. Write a suitable polite greeting.",answer:"bonjour",aliases:["bonjour madame","bonjour monsieur"],options:[],hint:"Use a general daytime hello.",why:"Bonjour suits a daytime shop interaction."},{prompt:"Complete a familiar exchange: Ça va ? — Bien, merci. Et ___ ?",answer:"toi",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the stressed form after et.",why:"Et toi returns a question to someone addressed with tu."},{prompt:"Complete a polite exchange: Vous allez bien ? — Très bien. Et ___ ?",answer:"vous",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Keep the same level of formality.",why:"Et vous matches polite/plural vous."},{prompt:"Write “See you tomorrow”.",answer:"à demain",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Tomorrow is demain.",why:"À demain refers to the next day."},{prompt:"Write “Have a good day”.",answer:"bonne journée",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"This is usually a parting wish.",why:"Bonne journée wishes someone a good day when leaving."},{prompt:"Write “not bad”.",answer:"pas mal",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Two words.",why:"Pas mal means not bad, not very bad."}]},task:{prompt:"Write two short exchanges: one with a classmate and one with a shop assistant. Include a greeting, a question, an answer and a goodbye.",model:`Salut ! Ça va ? — Oui, ça va bien, merci. Et toi ? — Très bien. À demain !
Bonjour, madame. Vous allez bien ? — Très bien, merci. Et vous ? — Bien, merci. Au revoir !`,english:`Hi! How are you? — Yes, I’m well, thank you. And you? — Very well. See you tomorrow!
Hello, madam. Are you well? — Very well, thank you. And you? — Well, thank you. Goodbye!`,checks:["I used toi in the familiar exchange.","I kept vous in the polite exchange.","My goodbye fits when we will next meet."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Vous allez bien ? Et toi ?",correct:"Vous allez bien ? Et vous ?",why:"Keep polite address consistent."},{wrong:"Bonjour ! (when leaving)",correct:"Au revoir ! / Bonne journée !",why:"Use a parting phrase when the conversation ends."}],tracks:[5],resources:[{material:"book",page:12,label:"Greeting dialogues"},{material:"workbook",page:3,label:"Greeting exercises"}],words:[{fr:"bonsoir",en:"good evening",note:"An evening hello."},{fr:"pas mal",en:"not bad",note:"A fixed response."},{fr:"très bien",en:"very well",note:"Très strengthens bien."}],listenTask:"Identify who greets someone, asks how they are, or says goodbye. Listen once without the transcript, then read the translated lines and repeat one exchange."},n={minutes:"30–40",objectives:["Spell a name using French letter names.","Distinguish common letter confusions.","Ask for spelling and repetition."],blocks:[{title:"Letter names are not word sounds",text:"The French alphabet has 26 letters. When spelling a name, use letter names. When reading a word, letters combine to make sounds; you cannot pronounce a word by simply joining its letter names.",examples:[{fr:"B-O-N-J-O-U-R",en:"The spelling of bonjour",why:"Spelling a word and saying the word are different activities."}]},{title:"Common English-to-French traps",text:"French e, i and u have different names and sounds. French g and j are easy to confuse if you expect English letter names. Listen to those letters individually in the word bank, then in the complete alphabet.",examples:[{fr:"G, J",en:"Two different letter names",why:"Repeat each after the model. Do not swap them because of English habits."}]},{title:"Accents and spelling instructions",text:"An accent is a mark on a letter. Say e accent aigu for é, e accent grave for è, and c cédille for ç. Deux l or deux n can describe a doubled letter, but spelling each letter separately is also clear.",examples:[{fr:"Ça s’écrit comment ?",en:"How is it spelled?",why:"A natural request when you hear a new name."},{fr:"Vous pouvez répéter, s’il vous plaît ?",en:"Can you repeat, please?",why:"Use this if you miss a letter."}]},{title:"Listen in two passes",text:"First write the letters you recognise. On a second listen, fill the gaps rather than starting again. Names can have unexpected spellings; follow the letters, not your guess about the name.",examples:[{fr:"L-I-N-A",en:"L-I-N-A → Lina",why:"Write one letter per sound; a spelled name is a sequence of letter names."}]}],pronunciation:[["French u","For the French u sound, keep the tongue near the position for ee and round the lips. It differs from ou, which is closer to the vowel in English food."],["G and J","Use the individual-letter buttons. G is pronounced with a sound like the beginning of the French word géant; J ends with the vowel sound in French j’ai."]],worked:[{prompt:"Someone spells L-Y-N-D-A. Which name should you write?",steps:["Record every letter in order.","The second letter is Y, not I.","Do not replace the unfamiliar spelling with Linda."],answer:"Lynda",english:"The name is Lynda."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"A name is spelled L-Y-N-D-A. Write the name.",answer:"Lynda",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The second letter is Y.",why:"Follow the letters rather than the more familiar spelling Linda."},{prompt:"A name is spelled O-S-C-A-R. Write it.",answer:"Oscar",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Five letters.",why:"The letters form Oscar."},{prompt:"Write the letter immediately after G in the alphabet.",answer:"H",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use alphabetical order.",why:"G is followed by H."},{prompt:"Write the letter immediately before J.",answer:"I",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the alphabet sequence.",why:"I comes before J."},{prompt:"Complete the spelling request: Ça s’écrit ___ ?",answer:"comment",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"This asks how.",why:"Comment means how."},{prompt:"Write the French name for the mark in é: accent ___ .",answer:"aigu",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"It is not the accent grave.",why:"É has an accent aigu."}]},task:{prompt:"Spell your first name and a fictional surname aloud. Then ask your imaginary partner how their name is spelled and ask them to repeat.",model:"Je m’appelle Lina Martin. L-I-N-A, M-A-R-T-I-N. Ça s’écrit comment ? Vous pouvez répéter, s’il vous plaît ?",english:"My name is Lina Martin. L-I-N-A, M-A-R-T-I-N. How is it spelled? Can you repeat, please?",checks:["I used French letter names.","I distinguished e, i and u by listening.","I can ask for spelling and repetition."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Reading every word as letter names",correct:"Use letter names only when spelling",why:"Word pronunciation follows sound patterns."},{wrong:"L-Y-N-D-A → Linda",correct:"L-Y-N-D-A → Lynda",why:"Trust the spoken spelling rather than a familiar name."}],tracks:[6,7],resources:[{material:"book",page:13,label:"Alphabet and names"},{material:"workbook",page:4,label:"Spelling names and cities"}],words:[],listenTask:"Listen to the alphabet, then write one spelled name before opening its transcript. Replay the letters you confuse."},t={minutes:"25–35",objectives:["Say and ask a name.","Use je/j’, moi, toi and vous.","Introduce another person."],blocks:[{title:"The basic name pattern",text:"Je m’appelle means my name is; literally, I call myself. Je is I. Before the vowel sound in appelle, me is shortened to m’. Learn je m’appelle as a unit.",examples:[{fr:"Je m’appelle Lina.",en:"My name is Lina.",why:"Your own name follows the phrase unchanged."},{fr:"Moi, c’est Sam.",en:"I’m Sam.",why:"A common conversational alternative."}]},{title:"Ask a familiar or polite question",text:"Tu t’appelles comment ? is familiar. Vous vous appelez comment ? is polite or plural. Comment means how; the full phrase asks a name.",examples:[{fr:"Comment tu t’appelles ?",en:"What’s your name?",why:"Another word order with the same familiar meaning."},{fr:"Comment vous vous appelez ?",en:"What is your name?",why:"Use with a person addressed politely."}]},{title:"Je is a subject; moi is stressed",text:"Je goes with a verb: je m’appelle. Moi stands alone or adds emphasis: moi, c’est Lina. After et, use moi/toi/vous rather than je/tu.",examples:[{fr:"Et toi ?",en:"And you?",why:"Familiar, referring back to a question."},{fr:"Elle s’appelle Anna.",en:"Her name is Anna.",why:"For another woman or girl: elle s’appelle. For a man or boy: il s’appelle."}]},{title:"Meeting someone",text:"Enchanté / enchantée means pleased to meet you. In writing, the ending agrees with the speaker. The usual pronunciation is the same.",examples:[{fr:"Enchantée !",en:"Nice to meet you!",why:"A feminine written form."}]}],pronunciation:[["Apostrophes join sounds","Do not pause inside m’appelle. The final e of appelle is normally silent."],["Same sound, different spelling","Enchanté and enchantée are normally pronounced alike. Spelling can show a distinction that you do not hear."]],worked:[{prompt:"Tell a classmate your name and return the question.",steps:["Use je m’appelle for yourself.","The setting is familiar, so choose toi after et.","Keep your own name after m’appelle."],answer:"Je m’appelle Alex. Et toi ?",english:"My name is Alex. And you?"}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Translate “My name is Lina”.",answer:"Je m’appelle Lina",aliases:["moi c’est Lina"],options:[],hint:"Use je m’appelle.",why:"Je m’appelle introduces your name."},{prompt:"Complete: ___, c’est Sam.",answer:"moi",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the stressed form of I.",why:"Moi can stand apart from a verb as a stressed pronoun."},{prompt:"Complete: Je m’appelle Lina. Et ___ ? (familiar)",answer:"toi",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"After et, use a stressed pronoun.",why:"Et toi returns the question familiarly."},{prompt:"Complete: Elle ___ Anna.",answer:"s’appelle",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the form for she.",why:"Il/elle s’appelle introduces another person’s name."},{prompt:"Complete the polite question: Comment vous vous ___ ?",answer:"appelez",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The vous form ends in -ez.",why:"Vous vous appelez is the polite/plural form."},{prompt:"Translate “His name is Paul”.",answer:"Il s’appelle Paul",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use il for he.",why:"Il s’appelle gives his name."}]},task:{prompt:"Introduce yourself to a classmate, ask their name, introduce them to someone else and say that you are pleased to meet them.",model:"Bonjour ! Je m’appelle Lina. Et toi ? — Moi, c’est Paul. — Enchantée ! Voici Paul. Il s’appelle Paul.",english:"Hello! My name is Lina. And you? — I’m Paul. — Nice to meet you! This is Paul. His name is Paul.",checks:["I used a complete name pattern.","I used toi or vous consistently.","I can give someone else’s name with il/elle s’appelle."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Je appelle Lina",correct:"Je m’appelle Lina",why:"The reflexive part m’ belongs to s’appeler."},{wrong:"Et tu ?",correct:"Et toi ?",why:"Use a stressed pronoun after et."}],tracks:[8],resources:[{material:"book",page:13,label:"Introductions"},{material:"workbook",page:5,label:"Je, moi, toi and vous"}],words:[],listenTask:"Listen for the name pattern, the returned question and the response to meeting someone. Repeat both parts of an exchange."},a={minutes:"35–45",objectives:["Recognise and write 0–31.","Build the teen and twenty numbers.","Hear number sounds in prices."],blocks:[{title:"Learn 0–16 first",text:"The numbers 0–16 need individual practice. Pay special attention to onze (11), douze (12), treize (13), quatorze (14), quinze (15) and seize (16). The complete word bank gives every number from zero to thirty-one.",examples:[{fr:"onze, douze, treize",en:"eleven, twelve, thirteen",why:"Practise recognising each number before building longer ones."}]},{title:"Build 17–19 and 22–29",text:"Dix-sept is ten-seven, or 17. The same pattern gives dix-huit and dix-neuf. After vingt (20), add the unit: vingt-deux, vingt-trois and so on.",examples:[{fr:"dix-huit",en:"18",why:"Dix + huit."},{fr:"vingt-quatre",en:"24",why:"Vingt + quatre."}]},{title:"Twenty-one and thirty-one",text:"Use et un in vingt et un and trente et un. Other numbers in the twenties do not take et: vingt-deux, not vingt et deux. Modern spelling may also connect the complete number with hyphens.",examples:[{fr:"trente et un",en:"31",why:"Trente + et + un."}]},{title:"Numbers change sound in context",text:"A final consonant can be silent alone or before a consonant but heard before a vowel. Deux euros links with a /z/ sound. In dix euros, dix also has a /z/ sound. Listen to the complete price.",examples:[{fr:"deux euros",en:"two euros",why:"The x links to euros with /z/."},{fr:"un euro",en:"one euro",why:"Listen for the linking n sound."}]}],pronunciation:[["Do not rely on English teens","Treize is 13, not 3. Contrast trois/treize, quatre/quatorze and cinq/quinze using the recording."],["Sound and spelling","The s you hear in six alone is not a separate written word. The sound can change in six euros or six personnes."]],worked:[{prompt:"Write 28 in French.",steps:["Identify the tens: 20 is vingt.","Identify the unit: 8 is huit.","Combine them without et."],answer:"vingt-huit",english:"28"}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Write 12 in French.",answer:"douze",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Learn 11–16 individually.",why:"Douze is 12; deux is 2."},{prompt:"Write 18 in French.",answer:"dix-huit",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Build ten + eight.",why:"Dix-huit means 10 + 8."},{prompt:"Write 21 in French.",answer:"vingt et un",aliases:["vingt-et-un"],options:[],hint:"This number uses et.",why:"Vingt et un is the special 20 + 1 pattern."},{prompt:"Write 24 in French.",answer:"vingt-quatre",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"No et is needed.",why:"Vingt + quatre gives 24."},{prompt:"Write 31 in French.",answer:"trente et un",aliases:["trente-et-un"],options:[],hint:"Use the same et un pattern as 21.",why:"Trente et un means 31."},{prompt:"Write the digits for quinze.",answer:"15",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"It is a teen number.",why:"Quinze is 15, while cinq is 5."},{prompt:"Write the digits for vingt-neuf.",answer:"29",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Twenty + nine.",why:"Vingt-neuf is 29."},{prompt:"What consonant sound links deux euros: n or z?",answer:"z",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Listen to track 10.",why:"The final x of deux makes a /z/ liaison before euros."}]},task:{prompt:"Say three fictional prices and the numbers 11–16 in mixed order. Write five numbers, cover the answers and say them again.",model:"Un café : deux euros. Un billet : quinze euros. Un livre : vingt et un euros. Onze, quinze, douze, seize, treize, quatorze.",english:"A coffee: two euros. A ticket: fifteen euros. A book: twenty-one euros. Eleven, fifteen, twelve, sixteen, thirteen, fourteen.",checks:["I can distinguish 2/12, 3/13 and 5/15.","I used et in 21 and 31.","I can understand a price without reading its digits."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"vingt et deux",correct:"vingt-deux",why:"Only 21 in this range uses et un."},{wrong:"quinze = 5",correct:"quinze = 15",why:"Cinq is 5. Quinze is 15."}],tracks:[9,10],resources:[{material:"book",page:14,label:"Numbers 0–31"},{material:"workbook",page:5,label:"Number practice"}],words:[],listenTask:"Write the numbers you recognise. Then compare the number alone with its sound before euros. Replay any teen number you confuse with a single digit."},s={minutes:"35–45",objectives:["Name weekdays and months.","Say a calendar date.","Distinguish today, tomorrow and the first of a month."],blocks:[{title:"Weekdays and months",text:"French weekday and month names normally start with lower-case letters. The week here begins with lundi, Monday. Learn the sequence, then practise out of order so you can recognise a date in speech.",examples:[{fr:"lundi, mardi, mercredi ; janvier, février, mars",en:"Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday; January, February, March",why:"These names use lower-case letters in ordinary French dates."}]},{title:"Say the day and the date",text:"Aujourd’hui means today; demain means tomorrow. To name the day, say Aujourd’hui, c’est lundi. To give a date, use le + number + month.",examples:[{fr:"Aujourd’hui, c’est mardi.",en:"Today is Tuesday.",why:"C’est means it is / that is."},{fr:"le deux décembre",en:"2 December",why:"The day comes before the month."}]},{title:"The first is special",text:"Use premier, not un, for the first day of a month. Other days use ordinary numbers: le deux, le trois, le vingt et un.",examples:[{fr:"le premier mai",en:"1 May",why:"Premier is the ordinal first."},{fr:"le vingt et un juin",en:"21 June",why:"Ordinary number + month."}]},{title:"One event versus a habit",text:"Lundi, j’ai un cours refers to a particular Monday. Le lundi can mean on Mondays as a habit. For now, practise dates with le and specific days without adding English-style on.",examples:[{fr:"Demain, c’est mercredi.",en:"Tomorrow is Wednesday.",why:"If today is Tuesday, the next day is Wednesday."}]}],pronunciation:[["Final consonants in dates","Listen to the full date rather than applying one rule to every number. Six, dix and other numbers can change sound before a month name."],["Repeat the month as a whole word","Août, juin and juillet need listening practice. Juin and juillet are different months: June and July."]],worked:[{prompt:"Today is Tuesday, 30 April. Say tomorrow’s day and date.",steps:["The day after mardi is mercredi.","April has 30 days; the next date is 1 May.","Use premier for the first day."],answer:"Demain, c’est mercredi, le premier mai.",english:"Tomorrow is Wednesday, 1 May."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Write “Monday” in French.",answer:"lundi",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The first day in this sequence.",why:"Lundi means Monday."},{prompt:"Today is mardi. Write tomorrow’s weekday.",answer:"mercredi",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Move one day forward.",why:"Mercredi follows mardi."},{prompt:"Write “1 May” in French, starting with le.",answer:"le premier mai",aliases:["le 1er mai"],options:[],hint:"Use premier for the first day.",why:"Calendar dates use le premier for day 1."},{prompt:"Write “2 December” in French, using words.",answer:"le deux décembre",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Article + day + month.",why:"Le deux décembre is the date pattern."},{prompt:"Write the French month after juin.",answer:"juillet",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"June is followed by July.",why:"Juillet is July, after juin."},{prompt:"Translate the single word “tomorrow”.",answer:"demain",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"It is not aujourd’hui.",why:"Demain is tomorrow; aujourd’hui is today."}]},task:{prompt:"Give today’s date, tomorrow’s date and the date of a fictional class event. Say each one, then write it in French.",model:"Aujourd’hui, c’est lundi, le deux décembre. Demain, c’est mardi, le trois décembre. Le cours, c’est le cinq décembre.",english:"Today is Monday, 2 December. Tomorrow is Tuesday, 3 December. The class is on 5 December. These are practice dates, not today’s actual date.",checks:["I used the correct weekday sequence.","I put the day before the month.","I used premier only for day 1."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"le un mai",correct:"le premier mai",why:"The first of the month uses premier."},{wrong:"Juin = July",correct:"Juin = June; juillet = July",why:"These similar-looking months have different meanings."}],tracks:[11],resources:[{material:"book",page:15,label:"Days and dates"},{material:"workbook",page:6,label:"Calendar exercises"}],words:[],listenTask:"Write the day and month you hear before checking the translated transcript. Notice that the date order is day then month."},i={minutes:"25–35",objectives:["Ask for repetition, spelling and a translation.","Say you do not understand.","Choose polite and familiar requests."],blocks:[{title:"Use a repair phrase immediately",text:"You do not have to wait until you understand everything. Ask the speaker to repeat, speak more slowly or spell a word. These phrases keep the conversation going.",examples:[{fr:"Vous pouvez répéter, s’il vous plaît ?",en:"Can you repeat, please?",why:"A polite request."},{fr:"Plus lentement, s’il vous plaît.",en:"More slowly, please.",why:"Lentement means slowly."}]},{title:"Ask for a word or its spelling",text:"Comment means how. En français means in French. To ask how to say an English word, put it into the gap in the phrase.",examples:[{fr:"Comment on dit “hello” en français ?",en:"How do you say “hello” in French?",why:"The reply is bonjour."},{fr:"Comment ça s’écrit ?",en:"How is it spelled?",why:"Use this when you know the sound but not the spelling."}]},{title:"A useful negative",text:"Je comprends means I understand. Je ne comprends pas means I do not understand. In standard written French, ne goes before the verb and pas after it. Before a vowel, ne becomes n’.",examples:[{fr:"Je ne comprends pas.",en:"I don’t understand.",why:"Ne + comprends + pas."}]},{title:"Choose one style of address",text:"S’il vous plaît is polite or plural please; s’il te plaît is familiar singular please. Excusez-moi matches vous; excuse-moi matches tu.",examples:[{fr:"Excusez-moi, je suis en retard.",en:"Excuse me, I’m late.",why:"Je suis means I am; en retard means late."}]}],pronunciation:[["Comprehension before speed","Practise one full repair phrase at a comfortable speed. The goal is to be understood, not to imitate fast speech immediately."],["The nasal ending in comprends","Avoid adding a clear English n after the vowel. Listen to the original phrase in track 12."]],worked:[{prompt:"You missed a word and want the teacher to repeat it politely.",steps:["Choose the repetition request, not the spelling request.","Use vous for a polite interaction.","Finish with s’il vous plaît."],answer:"Vous pouvez répéter, s’il vous plaît ?",english:"Can you repeat, please?"}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Complete: Je ne comprends ___ .",answer:"pas",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Written negation has two parts.",why:"Ne… pas surrounds the verb."},{prompt:"Complete the request “More slowly, please”: Plus ___, s’il vous plaît.",answer:"lentement",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the adverb for slowly.",why:"Lentement means slowly."},{prompt:"Complete: Vous pouvez ___, s’il vous plaît ? (“repeat”)",answer:"répéter",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the infinitive after pouvez.",why:"Répéter means to repeat."},{prompt:"Complete: Comment ça ___ ? (“How is it spelled?”)",answer:"s’écrit",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The verb comes from écrire.",why:"Comment ça s’écrit asks about spelling."},{prompt:"Complete familiar please: s’il ___ plaît.",answer:"te",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Choose the familiar singular form.",why:"S’il te plaît goes with tu."},{prompt:"Complete polite excuse me: ___-moi.",answer:"excusez",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the vous form.",why:"Excusez-moi is polite/plural; excuse-moi is familiar."}]},task:{prompt:"Rehearse being lost in class. Say that you do not understand, ask for repetition, then ask how a word is spelled.",model:"Excusez-moi. Je ne comprends pas. Vous pouvez répéter, s’il vous plaît ? Plus lentement, s’il vous plaît. Comment ça s’écrit ?",english:"Excuse me. I don’t understand. Can you repeat, please? More slowly, please. How is it spelled?",checks:["I can ask for help without reading.","I kept the request polite throughout.","I used both ne and pas in my written negative."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Je comprends pas (formal written practice)",correct:"Je ne comprends pas",why:"Ne is often omitted in conversation, but practise the complete written pattern."},{wrong:"Vous… s’il te plaît",correct:"Vous… s’il vous plaît",why:"Keep the style of address consistent."}],tracks:[12],resources:[{material:"book",page:16,label:"Ask for help"},{material:"workbook",page:6,label:"Classroom situations"}],words:[{fr:"plus lentement",en:"more slowly",note:"A useful request."},{fr:"je comprends",en:"I understand",note:"Comprendre: je comprends."},{fr:"répéter",en:"to repeat",note:"An infinitive; useful after vous pouvez."}],listenTask:"For each phrase, identify its purpose: repetition, spelling, asking for a French word or saying you do not understand."},r={"first-words":{minutes:"20–30",objectives:["Recognise a few familiar French words.","Use a polite request without translating word for word.","Tell the difference between a word’s spelling and sound."],blocks:[{title:"Start with meaning, then sound",text:"French spelling often looks familiar, but pronunciation can differ. You do not need to understand every word before using a short useful phrase. Listen, read the meaning, then repeat.",examples:[{fr:"Bonjour !",en:"Hello!",why:"A daytime greeting; it also works when entering a shop."},{fr:"Un café, s’il vous plaît.",en:"A coffee, please.",why:"Un is a masculine indefinite article. Learn the whole polite request."}]},{title:"Learn nouns with their articles",text:"A noun names a person, place or thing. French nouns have a grammatical gender. Un means a/an with a masculine noun; une does the same with a feminine noun. Gender does not describe whether an object is male or female.",examples:[{fr:"un restaurant",en:"a restaurant",why:"Restaurant is masculine."},{fr:"une gare",en:"a railway station",why:"Gare is feminine. Learn une together with gare."}]},{title:"Small marks change words",text:"An acute accent is the mark in café. A cedilla is the mark under ç in français. These marks help indicate pronunciation; they are part of the spelling, not decoration.",examples:[{fr:"un café",en:"a coffee / a café",why:"The final é is pronounced; the final t of restaurant is usually silent."}]},{title:"Your first conversation routine",text:"Greet first, make a request, say thank you, then say goodbye. The course will explain the grammar later; for now, use these useful chunks.",examples:[{fr:"Bonjour. Un café, s’il vous plaît. Merci. Au revoir.",en:"Hello. A coffee, please. Thank you. Goodbye.",why:"Each short part has its own purpose."}]}],pronunciation:[["Listen before guessing","In café, pronounce the final é. In restaurant, do not add an English-sounding final t. Use the recording as your model."],["French rhythm","Say a short phrase smoothly. Avoid giving every little word a separate strong English stress."]],worked:[{prompt:"Ask politely for a coffee.",steps:["Start with un café: a coffee.","Add s’il vous plaît: please in a polite interaction.","Greet the person before the request."],answer:"Bonjour. Un café, s’il vous plaît.",english:"Hello. A coffee, please."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Write the daytime greeting meaning “hello”.",answer:"bonjour",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"It starts with bon-.",why:"Bonjour is the standard daytime greeting."},{prompt:"Complete: un café, ___ (“please”, polite).",answer:"s’il vous plaît",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Learn this as a whole chunk.",why:"S’il vous plaît is polite or plural please."},{prompt:"Write “thank you”.",answer:"merci",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"One short word.",why:"Merci thanks the other person."},{prompt:"Which article goes with the feminine noun gare: un or une?",answer:"une",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Match the noun’s gender.",why:"Une is the feminine indefinite article."},{prompt:"Which article goes with restaurant: un or une?",answer:"un",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Restaurant is masculine.",why:"A masculine singular noun takes un."},{prompt:"Write the goodbye meaning “goodbye”.",answer:"au revoir",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Two words.",why:"Au revoir is a goodbye, while bonjour starts an interaction."}]},task:{prompt:"Rehearse entering a café. Greet the server, request one coffee politely, thank them and say goodbye. Write your four phrases, then say them without looking.",model:"Bonjour ! Un café, s’il vous plaît. Merci. Au revoir !",english:"Hello! A coffee, please. Thank you. Goodbye!",checks:["I greeted the person first.","I used the whole polite request.","I can say the four phrases without reading."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Bonjour = goodbye",correct:"Bonjour = hello; au revoir = goodbye",why:"Choose a phrase by its purpose."},{wrong:"une restaurant",correct:"un restaurant",why:"Restaurant is a masculine noun; learn its article with it."}],tracks:[2,3,4],resources:[{material:"book",page:11,label:"Familiar words and places"}],words:[{fr:"une gare",en:"a railway station",note:"Feminine; plural: des gares."},{fr:"merci",en:"thank you",note:"A fixed expression."},{fr:"au revoir",en:"goodbye",note:"A fixed expression, pronounced as a phrase."},{fr:"chocolat",en:"chocolate",note:"Masculine: le chocolat (chocolate), un chocolat (a chocolate/hot chocolate). Plural: des chocolats."},{fr:"restaurant",en:"restaurant",note:"Masculine: un restaurant; plural: des restaurants."},{fr:"cinéma",en:"cinema",note:"Masculine: le cinéma (cinema as an interest), un cinéma (a cinema/theatre). Plural: des cinémas."},{fr:"croissant",en:"croissant",note:"Masculine: un croissant; plural: des croissants."},{fr:"train",en:"train",note:"Masculine: un train; plural: des trains."},{fr:"toilettes",en:"toilets / restroom",note:"Feminine plural: les toilettes, meaning the restroom/toilets."}],listenTask:"Listen for familiar French words and one polite request. Track 2 deliberately includes several languages: the task is recognising French, not understanding every language."},greetings:e,alphabet:n,introductions:t,numbers:a,dates:s,classroom:i,"u1-introduce":{minutes:"60–90",objectives:["Introduce a person with name, nationality and age.","Use être and avoir.","Talk about interests with the correct articles."],blocks:[{title:"Subject pronouns",text:"Je = I, tu = familiar you, il = he/it, elle = she/it, nous = we, vous = polite/plural you, ils = they (masculine or mixed), elles = they (all feminine). On often means we in everyday conversation, but its verb has the il/elle form.",examples:[{fr:"Nous sommes français. On est français.",en:"We are French. We are French.",why:"Nous takes sommes; conversational on takes est, even when it means we."}]},{title:"Être: to be",text:"Learn the present forms together: je suis, tu es, il/elle/on est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils/elles sont. French normally needs the subject pronoun; do not leave je out when saying who you are.",examples:[{fr:"Je suis italien.",en:"I’m Italian.",why:"Je + suis + a nationality adjective."},{fr:"Nous sommes français.",en:"We are French.",why:"Nous takes sommes, not sont."}]},{title:"Avoir: to have—and age",text:"Avoir: j’ai, tu as, il/elle/on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils/elles ont. French expresses age with avoir + number + ans, literally to have a number of years.",examples:[{fr:"J’ai vingt-trois ans.",en:"I’m 23 years old.",why:"Use avoir, not être."},{fr:"Tu as quel âge ?",en:"How old are you?",why:"Quel âge means what age."}]},{title:"Nationality agreement",text:"Nationality adjectives usually use lower-case letters. Add -e for many feminine forms: français/française, allemand/allemande. -ien becomes -ienne: italien/italienne. Belge, suisse and tchèque have the same singular form for both genders. Plural adjectives normally add -s.",examples:[{fr:"Il est italien. Elle est italienne.",en:"He is Italian. She is Italian.",why:"The adjective follows the person’s gender."}]},{title:"Interests need articles",text:"With a general interest, use aimer + le/la/l’/les + noun. Le is masculine singular; la feminine singular; les plural. L’ replaces le or la before a vowel or silent h.",examples:[{fr:"J’aime la musique et le cinéma.",en:"I like music and cinema.",why:"Keep the articles even though English omits them."},{fr:"Elle aime l’art et les langues.",en:"She likes art and languages.",why:"L’ before a vowel; les with a plural noun."}]},{title:"Numbers 32–69",text:"Build the tens: trente (30), quarante (40), cinquante (50), soixante (60). Add units as with the twenties. Use et un for 41, 51 and 61.",examples:[{fr:"quarante-quatre",en:"44",why:"Quarante + quatre."},{fr:"soixante et un",en:"61",why:"The et un pattern."}]}],pronunciation:[["Listen for changing feminine endings","Français/française differ because the feminine makes a final consonant audible. Espagnol/espagnole normally sound the same because masculine espagnol already pronounces its l."],["Rhythmic groups","Give each meaningful group one final stress: Je m’appelle Lina / j’ai vingt-trois ans. Do not assign every written word its own heavy stress."]],worked:[{prompt:"Introduce Anna: Italian woman, 28, likes music.",steps:["Use elle est for nationality.","Make italien feminine: italienne.","Use elle a for age, and keep la before musique."],answer:"Elle s’appelle Anna. Elle est italienne. Elle a vingt-huit ans. Elle aime la musique.",english:"Her name is Anna. She’s Italian. She’s 28. She likes music."},{prompt:"Choose the article before histoire.",steps:["Histoire is singular and feminine.","Its h is silent, so the noun starts with a vowel sound.","La becomes l’ before that sound."],answer:"J’aime l’histoire.",english:"I like history."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Complete “I am French” (woman): Je ___ française.",answer:"suis",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use être with je.",why:"Être: je suis."},{prompt:"Translate “I am 23 years old”, using digits.",answer:"J’ai 23 ans",aliases:["j’ai vingt-trois ans"],options:[],hint:"Age uses avoir.",why:"French says j’ai… ans, literally I have… years."},{prompt:"Complete: Elle est ___ . (Italian woman)",answer:"italienne",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"-ien becomes -ienne.",why:"Italienne agrees with elle."},{prompt:"Complete: Nous ___ belges.",answer:"sommes",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the nous form of être.",why:"Nous sommes means we are."},{prompt:"Complete: Vous ___ quel âge ?",answer:"avez",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use avoir with vous.",why:"Vous avez asks politely or plurally how old someone is."},{prompt:"Complete with an article: J’aime ___ musique.",answer:"la",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Musique is feminine singular.",why:"General interests keep the definite article."},{prompt:"Complete: Il aime ___ langues.",answer:"les",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Langues is plural.",why:"Les is the plural definite article."},{prompt:"Complete: Tu aimes ___ art ?",answer:"l’",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Art begins with a vowel.",why:"Le becomes l’ before art."},{prompt:"Write 44 in French.",answer:"quarante-quatre",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Forty + four.",why:"Quarante is 40; quatre is 4."},{prompt:"Complete: Ils ___ vingt ans.",answer:"ont",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Age uses avoir; the subject is ils.",why:"Ils ont is the they form of avoir."}]},task:{prompt:"Introduce yourself and a fictional classmate. Give each person’s name, nationality, age and two interests. Then ask your classmate one question.",model:"Je m’appelle Alex. Je suis belge. J’ai vingt-trois ans. J’aime la musique et le cinéma. Mon amie s’appelle Anna. Elle est italienne. Elle a vingt-huit ans. Elle aime l’art et les langues. Tu as quel âge ?",english:"My name is Alex. I’m Belgian. I’m 23. I like music and cinema. My friend’s name is Anna. She’s Italian. She’s 28. She likes art and languages. How old are you?",checks:["I used avoir for both ages.","I matched the nationality adjectives to each person.","I kept articles with interests.","I can say the introduction without reading every word."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Je suis 23 ans.",correct:"J’ai 23 ans.",why:"Age uses avoir."},{wrong:"Elle est italien.",correct:"Elle est italienne.",why:"The nationality adjective agrees with elle."},{wrong:"J’aime musique.",correct:"J’aime la musique.",why:"General interests take the definite article."}],tracks:[13,14,16,17,18,19,20],resources:[{material:"book",page:18,label:"Names, nationalities and ages"},{material:"book",page:20,label:"Interests and articles"},{material:"workbook",page:7,label:"Nationality agreement"},{material:"workbook",page:8,label:"Articles"},{material:"workbook",page:9,label:"Interests and numbers"}],words:[{fr:"un âge",en:"an age",note:"Masculine; l’âge with a definite article. Plural: les âges."},{fr:"une nationalité",en:"a nationality",note:"Feminine; plural: des nationalités."},{fr:"un pays",en:"a country",note:"Masculine; plural spelling is also pays."},{fr:"une langue",en:"a language",note:"Feminine; plural: des langues."},{fr:"l’art",en:"art",note:"Masculine; le becomes l’."},{fr:"l’histoire",en:"history",note:"Feminine; la becomes l’ before silent h."},{fr:"trente",en:"30",note:"Add units: trente-deux = 32."},{fr:"quarante",en:"40",note:"Quarante et un = 41."},{fr:"cinquante",en:"50",note:"Cinquante et un = 51."},{fr:"soixante",en:"60",note:"Soixante-neuf = 69."},{fr:"le cinéma",en:"cinema",note:"Masculine; plural: les cinémas when referring to movie theatres."},{fr:"les langues",en:"languages",note:"Feminine plural; singular: une langue."}],listenTask:"Make one row per speaker: name, nationality, age and interests. The sound tracks help you compare adjective endings and rhythm; do not try to memorise every dialogue at once."},"u1-identity":{minutes:"60–90",objectives:["Give birthplace and residence.","Ask for identity and contact information.","Understand numbers 70–100 and phone numbers."],blocks:[{title:"Cities and countries use different prepositions",text:"Use à with a city: à Paris, à Toronto. With countries, use en for feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au for masculine countries beginning with a consonant, and aux for plural countries.",examples:[{fr:"J’habite à Toronto, au Canada.",en:"I live in Toronto, Canada.",why:"City: à. Masculine consonant-initial country: au."},{fr:"Elle habite en France et il habite en Iran.",en:"She lives in France and he lives in Iran.",why:"La France is feminine; l’Iran is masculine but begins with a vowel."}]},{title:"Birthplace is not residence",text:"Je suis né / née means I was born; j’habite means I live. Né is the masculine written form and née the feminine. A biography may give different cities for birth and current residence.",examples:[{fr:"Je suis née à Paris, en France. Maintenant, j’habite au Canada.",en:"I was born in Paris, France. Now I live in Canada.",why:"Née describes a woman in this example."}]},{title:"Questions with quel",text:"Quel agrees with the following noun: quel âge, quelle ville, quels loisirs, quelles langues. It does not agree with the person answering. Quel est ton nom ? asks your name; Quelles langues tu parles ? asks which languages you speak.",examples:[{fr:"Quelle est ta date de naissance ?",en:"What is your birth date?",why:"Date is feminine singular, so quelle."},{fr:"Quels loisirs tu aimes ?",en:"Which leisure activities do you like?",why:"Loisirs is masculine plural, so quels."}]},{title:"Forms and contacts",text:"Prénom is first name; nom is surname in a form. Adresse can mean a postal address, while adresse mail means email address. Un numéro de téléphone is a telephone number. Use fictional contact details when practising.",examples:[{fr:"Nom : Martin. Prénom : Camille.",en:"Surname: Martin. First name: Camille.",why:"Do not reverse these labels."}]},{title:"Numbers 70–100",text:"In the France-based pattern used here, 70 is soixante-dix (60 + 10); 71 is soixante et onze. 80 is quatre-vingts (4 × 20); 81 is quatre-vingt-un, without et or final s on vingt. 90 is quatre-vingt-dix; 99 is quatre-vingt-dix-neuf; 100 is cent. Belgium and Switzerland use some different forms, such as septante and nonante.",examples:[{fr:"soixante et onze ; quatre-vingt-un ; quatre-vingt-dix",en:"seventy-one; eighty-one; ninety",why:"71 contains et; 81 does not. In 90, dix is added to eighty."}]},{title:"Birth years beyond one hundred",text:"Mille means one thousand. Deux mille is 2000; deux mille un is 2001. For 1999, say mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf (1000 + 900 + 99). You can write the year in digits on forms; practise saying your chosen fictional year in short parts.",examples:[{fr:"le douze mai deux mille",en:"12 May 2000",why:"Day + month + year. The spoken year follows the month."}]},{title:"Telephone numbers and linking sounds",text:"French domestic phone numbers are commonly spoken in pairs. Preserve all digits and the initial zero. Before a vowel, en can link with /n/ and aux with /z/: en Italie, aux États-Unis. Au does not add a linking consonant.",examples:[{fr:"06 12 34 56 78",en:"zero six, twelve, thirty-four, fifty-six, seventy-eight",why:"Read pairs: zéro six, douze, trente-quatre, cinquante-six, soixante-dix-huit. The written initial zero matters."}]}],pronunciation:[["Keep pairs of digits together","Write spaces between pairs while listening to phone numbers. You can ask for one pair to be repeated."],["Country liaison","Compare en France with en Italie. A separate English n is not added to every word ending in n; listen to the full phrase."]],worked:[{prompt:"Write “She lives in the United States”.",steps:["Use elle habite for she lives.","The country name is plural: les États-Unis.","Plural countries take aux."],answer:"Elle habite aux États-Unis.",english:"She lives in the United States."},{prompt:"Ask which languages someone speaks.",steps:["The noun is langues: feminine plural.","Choose quelles, not quel or quels.","Use the familiar tu form of parler."],answer:"Quelles langues tu parles ?",english:"Which languages do you speak?"}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Complete: J’habite ___ Paris.",answer:"à",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Paris is a city.",why:"Cities take à."},{prompt:"Complete: Elle habite ___ France.",answer:"en",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"France is feminine.",why:"A feminine country takes en."},{prompt:"Complete: Il habite ___ Canada.",answer:"au",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Canada is masculine and begins with a consonant.",why:"Masculine consonant-initial countries take au."},{prompt:"Complete: Nous habitons ___ États-Unis.",answer:"aux",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The country name is plural.",why:"Plural countries take aux."},{prompt:"Complete: Tu habites ___ Iran ?",answer:"en",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Iran starts with a vowel.",why:"A masculine vowel-initial country also takes en."},{prompt:"Complete: ___ langues tu parles ?",answer:"quelles",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Langues is feminine plural.",why:"Quelles agrees with langues."},{prompt:"Complete: ___ âge tu as ?",answer:"quel",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Âge is masculine singular.",why:"Quel agrees with âge."},{prompt:"Which French form label means “first name”?",answer:"prénom",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Nom and prénom are different labels.",why:"Prénom means first name; nom is surname on a form."},{prompt:"Write the digits for quatre-vingt-dix.",answer:"90",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Four twenties plus ten.",why:"80 + 10 gives 90."},{prompt:"Write 80 in French.",answer:"quatre-vingts",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"This round number keeps the s.",why:"Quatre-vingts is 80; the s disappears when another number follows."}]},task:{prompt:"Create a fictional registration profile: surname, first name, birth date, birthplace, residence, languages and telephone. Then ask three questions about another person’s profile.",model:"Nom : Martin. Prénom : Camille. Je suis née le douze mai deux mille à Paris, en France. J’habite à Toronto, au Canada. Je parle français et anglais. Mon numéro est le 06 12 34 56 78. Quelle est ta date de naissance ? Tu habites dans quelle ville ? Quelles langues tu parles ?",english:"Surname: Martin. First name: Camille. I was born on 12 May 2000 in Paris, France. I live in Toronto, Canada. I speak French and English. My number is 06 12 34 56 78. What is your birth date? Which city do you live in? Which languages do you speak?",checks:["I separated birthplace from residence.","I used the correct country prepositions.","I matched quel to the noun.","My contact details are fictional and preserve every digit."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"à France",correct:"en France",why:"Use en with the feminine country France."},{wrong:"Quel langues ?",correct:"Quelles langues ?",why:"Langues is feminine plural."},{wrong:"Prénom = surname",correct:"Prénom = first name",why:"Nom is surname in a registration form."}],tracks:[21,22,24,25,26,27,28,29],resources:[{material:"book",page:24,label:"Biographies"},{material:"book",page:25,label:"Registration"},{material:"book",page:26,label:"Languages and contacts"},{material:"workbook",page:10,label:"Cities and countries"},{material:"workbook",page:11,label:"Quel questions"},{material:"workbook",page:12,label:"Identity vocabulary"},{material:"workbook",page:13,label:"A registration profile"}],words:[{fr:"un nom",en:"a surname / a name",note:"Masculine; plural: des noms. On forms, usually surname."},{fr:"un prénom",en:"a first name",note:"Masculine; plural: des prénoms."},{fr:"une ville",en:"a city / town",note:"Feminine; plural: des villes."},{fr:"une adresse",en:"an address",note:"Feminine; plural: des adresses."},{fr:"une adresse mail",en:"an email address",note:"Feminine; plural: des adresses mail."},{fr:"Je parle anglais.",en:"I speak English.",note:"Language names take no article directly after parler."},{fr:"soixante-dix",en:"70",note:"60 + 10."},{fr:"quatre-vingts",en:"80",note:"Keep the s only when no further number follows."},{fr:"quatre-vingt-un",en:"81",note:"No et, and no final s on vingt."},{fr:"cent",en:"100",note:"Learn the number as a whole word."},{fr:"mille",en:"1,000",note:"Mille does not take a plural s."},{fr:"deux mille",en:"2,000",note:"Also the year 2000."}],listenTask:"Use separate columns for surname, first name, birth date, birthplace, residence, languages and phone. Do not mistake a question for the answer that follows it."},"u1-documents":{minutes:"30–45",objectives:["Name common identity and travel documents.","Understand polite requests for documents.","Combine your Unit 1 skills in a short profile."],blocks:[{title:"Match the document to its purpose",text:"Un passeport is a passport; une carte d’identité an identity card; un permis de conduire a driving licence. Une carte d’étudiant identifies a student; une carte de bibliothèque is a library card. Un titre de transport is a travel ticket or pass, not necessarily a named identity card.",examples:[{fr:"C’est une carte de bibliothèque.",en:"It is a library card.",why:"The noun carte is feminine, so use une. De bibliothèque tells you its purpose."}]},{title:"Understand a request before answering",text:"Votre means your for polite or plural vous. Vous avez… ? means do you have…? An intonation question can use the same word order as a statement.",examples:[{fr:"Votre passeport, s’il vous plaît.",en:"Your passport, please.",why:"A shortened polite request."},{fr:"Vous avez une carte d’étudiant ?",en:"Do you have a student card?",why:"Vous avez + noun becomes a question through context and intonation."}]},{title:"Possession and a useful negative",text:"J’ai means I have. Je n’ai pas means I do not have. Before the vowel in ai, ne becomes n’. A basic negative with an indefinite article normally uses de/d’: je n’ai pas de passeport.",examples:[{fr:"Oui, j’ai un passeport.",en:"Yes, I have a passport.",why:"A positive statement."},{fr:"Non, je n’ai pas de permis de conduire.",en:"No, I do not have a driving licence.",why:"Ne… pas surrounds ai; un becomes de."}]},{title:"Present a person from a source",text:"For an artist or fictional person, give the name, nationality, birthplace/date and one work or interest. Distinguish what the source says from your own guesses. You can complete Unit 1’s course task without researching a real person.",examples:[{fr:"Elle s’appelle Lina. Elle est française. Elle aime le cinéma.",en:"Her name is Lina. She is French. She likes cinema.",why:"This fictional example combines a name, nationality and interest using patterns you already know."}]}],pronunciation:[["Carte and permis","The final e of carte is normally silent; pronounce its t. The final s of permis is normally silent. Listen to the document request in track 30."],["Polite requests","Keep s’il vous plaît together. You do not need to copy a fast speaker’s speed to sound polite."]],worked:[{prompt:"Politely ask someone for a passport.",steps:["Choose the document: un passeport.","Votre makes it their passport in a polite request.","Add s’il vous plaît."],answer:"Votre passeport, s’il vous plaît.",english:"Your passport, please."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Name the document meaning “passport”, including un.",answer:"un passeport",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"A masculine document noun.",why:"Un passeport is a passport."},{prompt:"Name the document meaning “identity card”, including une.",answer:"une carte d’identité",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use carte + de + identité.",why:"Une carte d’identité is an identity card."},{prompt:"Complete: un permis de ___ .",answer:"conduire",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"This document lets you drive.",why:"Un permis de conduire is a driving licence."},{prompt:"Complete polite “your passport”: ___ passeport.",answer:"votre",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the possessive corresponding to vous.",why:"Votre is polite or plural your."},{prompt:"Complete: Non, je n’___ pas de passeport.",answer:"ai",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The verb is avoir.",why:"Je n’ai pas is I do not have."},{prompt:"Complete: une carte de ___ . (“library card”)",answer:"bibliothèque",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The library lends books.",why:"Une carte de bibliothèque is a library card."}]},task:{prompt:"Role-play a library or travel desk. Ask for a document politely, answer whether you have it, then give your fictional name, nationality and age.",model:"Bonjour. Vous avez une carte de bibliothèque ? — Oui, j’ai une carte. — Votre nom, s’il vous plaît ? — Je m’appelle Camille Martin. Je suis belge. J’ai vingt-trois ans.",english:"Hello. Do you have a library card? — Yes, I have a card. — Your name, please? — My name is Camille Martin. I’m Belgian. I’m 23.",checks:["I chose a document that fits the setting.","I used vous/votre consistently.","I can give my identity without copying the model."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"votre = our",correct:"votre = your; notre = our",why:"Votre belongs to vous."},{wrong:"Je n’ai pas un passeport. (basic possession)",correct:"Je n’ai pas de passeport.",why:"For this ordinary negative possession pattern, use de."}],tracks:[30],resources:[{material:"book",page:29,label:"Present an artist"},{material:"book",page:30,label:"Documents"},{material:"workbook",page:14,label:"Unit 1 grammar review"},{material:"workbook",page:15,label:"Unit 1 vocabulary review"},{material:"workbook",page:16,label:"Unit 1 DELF"},{material:"workbook",page:17,label:"Unit 1 speaking and writing"}],words:[{fr:"le passeport",en:"passport",note:"Masculine; plural: les passeports."},{fr:"la carte d’identité",en:"identity card",note:"Feminine; plural: les cartes d’identité."},{fr:"le permis de conduire",en:"driving licence",note:"Masculine; plural: les permis de conduire (permis keeps the same spelling)."},{fr:"le titre de transport",en:"travel ticket or pass",note:"Masculine; plural: les titres de transport."},{fr:"la carte d’étudiant",en:"student card",note:"Feminine; plural: les cartes d’étudiant."},{fr:"la carte de bibliothèque",en:"library card",note:"Feminine; plural: les cartes de bibliothèque."}],listenTask:"Identify which document is requested in each situation. Repeat the whole polite request, then answer oui or non with a complete phrase."},"u2-home":{minutes:"60–90",objectives:["Describe where you live and who lives with you.","Conjugate regular -er verbs and make negatives.","Discuss activities and preferences."],blocks:[{title:"Introduce something, then identify it",text:"Un/une/des introduces an unspecified noun; le/la/l’/les identifies something known or refers to a general category. Use the gender and number of the noun.",examples:[{fr:"J’habite dans un quartier. Le quartier est calme.",en:"I live in a neighbourhood. The neighbourhood is quiet.",why:"First introduction: un. The identified place afterward: le."},{fr:"J’aime les films.",en:"I like films.",why:"General preferences use the definite article."}]},{title:"Describe a home",text:"Habiter means to live. Use à + city, dans + a dwelling or neighbourhood, avec + a person. Il y a means there is / there are. A simple adjective often follows the noun.",examples:[{fr:"J’habite à Nice, dans un appartement, avec une amie.",en:"I live in Nice, in an apartment, with a friend.",why:"Three phrases answer where and with whom."},{fr:"Il y a une plage près de chez moi.",en:"There is a beach near my home.",why:"Près de chez moi means near where I live."}]},{title:"Regular -er verbs",text:"Remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent: je parle, tu parles, il/elle/on parle, nous parlons, vous parlez, ils/elles parlent. Habiter, aimer, adorer, détester, travailler and étudier follow this pattern. Je becomes j’ before a vowel or silent h.",examples:[{fr:"Nous habitons à Lyon.",en:"We live in Lyon.",why:"Nous takes -ons."},{fr:"Elles aiment la musique.",en:"They like music.",why:"Elles takes -ent, which is silent."}]},{title:"Negation",text:"Put ne/n’ before the conjugated verb and pas after it. Before a vowel, use n’. In everyday speech, ne is often left out; practise the complete written pattern.",examples:[{fr:"Je n’aime pas le ski.",en:"I don’t like skiing.",why:"N’ before aime; pas after it."},{fr:"Nous ne parlons pas allemand.",en:"We do not speak German.",why:"Ne + parlons + pas."}]},{title:"Preferences and activities",text:"J’aime means I like, j’adore means I love and je déteste means I hate. Use a noun with its article or an infinitive without an article.",examples:[{fr:"J’aime la natation. J’aime nager.",en:"I like swimming. I like to swim.",why:"La natation is a noun; nager is a verb infinitive."},{fr:"Je joue du piano.",en:"I play the piano.",why:"Learn jouer du piano and jouer de la guitare as useful phrases."}]},{title:"Ask a follow-up question",text:"Tu habites où ? asks where you live. Tu habites avec qui ? asks with whom. Tu aimes quels sports ? asks which sports. In a reply, on often means we but uses a singular verb.",examples:[{fr:"On habite dans une rue calme.",en:"We live on a quiet street.",why:"On takes habite, not habitons."}]}],pronunciation:[["Silent -er endings","Parle, parles and parlent have the same verb sound. Il parle and ils parlent may sound the same; context tells you the number."],["Liaison can distinguish subjects","Il aime and ils aiment differ because ils links to aiment with /z/. Do not pronounce the -ent verb ending."]],worked:[{prompt:"Make “Nous aimons le ski” negative.",steps:["The conjugated verb is aimons.","Put ne before it; because aimons begins with a vowel, shorten ne to n’.","Put pas after aimons and keep le ski."],answer:"Nous n’aimons pas le ski.",english:"We do not like skiing."},{prompt:"Conjugate parler with vous.",steps:["Remove -er: parl-.","Vous takes -ez.","Combine the stem and ending."],answer:"Vous parlez français.",english:"You speak French."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Complete: Nous ___ à Lyon. (habiter)",answer:"habitons",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Nous takes -ons.",why:"Habit- + -ons gives habitons."},{prompt:"Complete: Vous ___ français. (parler)",answer:"parlez",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Vous takes -ez.",why:"Parl- + -ez gives parlez."},{prompt:"Complete: Ils ___ la musique. (aimer)",answer:"aiment",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Ils takes -ent.",why:"Aim- + -ent gives aiment; do not pronounce -ent."},{prompt:"Complete: Tu ___ dans un restaurant. (travailler)",answer:"travailles",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Tu takes -es.",why:"Travaill- + -es gives travailles."},{prompt:"Make this negative: Je parle allemand.",answer:"Je ne parle pas allemand",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Place ne and pas around parle.",why:"Ne comes before the verb; pas follows it."},{prompt:"Make this negative: J’aime le ski.",answer:"Je n’aime pas le ski",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Ne becomes n’ before a vowel.",why:"The negative is je n’aime pas, with the article le retained."},{prompt:"Complete: J’habite dans ___ appartement. (an)",answer:"un",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Appartement is masculine.",why:"Use un to introduce an unspecified apartment."},{prompt:"Complete “I like swimming”: J’aime ___ natation.",answer:"la",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Natation is a feminine noun.",why:"A general interest expressed as a noun takes the definite article."},{prompt:"Complete “We live…” using on: On ___ à Nice.",answer:"habite",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"On takes the il/elle form.",why:"On means we here, but takes a singular verb."},{prompt:"Complete: Je joue ___ piano.",answer:"du",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Learn the complete musical-instrument phrase.",why:"Jouer du piano means play the piano."}]},task:{prompt:"Describe your home or a fictional home in five sentences. Include city, dwelling, neighbourhood, who lives there, one activity you like and one you do not like. Ask a follow-up question.",model:"J’habite à Nice dans un appartement avec une amie. Notre rue est calme. Le quartier est sympa. J’aime la natation et je joue du piano. Je n’aime pas le ski. Et toi, tu habites où ?",english:"I live in Nice in an apartment with a friend. Our street is quiet. The neighbourhood is nice. I like swimming and I play the piano. I don’t like skiing. And you, where do you live?",checks:["I used à/dans/avec for the correct purposes.","My verb endings match their subjects.","My written negative includes ne/n’ and pas.","I can ask a follow-up question."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"Nous habite",correct:"Nous habitons",why:"Nous takes the -ons verb ending."},{wrong:"Ils parlent: pronounce every written letter",correct:"Ils parlent: -ent is silent",why:"Verb endings can differ in writing while sounding identical."},{wrong:"J’aime pas le ski. (standard written practice)",correct:"Je n’aime pas le ski.",why:"Practise the complete written negative."}],tracks:[31,33,34,35,36,37,38],resources:[{material:"book",page:32,label:"Your home"},{material:"book",page:34,label:"Preferences and -er verbs"},{material:"workbook",page:19,label:"Articles"},{material:"workbook",page:20,label:"Verbs and negatives"},{material:"workbook",page:21,label:"Places and leisure"}],words:[{fr:"une maison",en:"a house",note:"Feminine; plural: des maisons."},{fr:"un quartier calme",en:"a quiet neighbourhood",note:"Quartier is masculine; plural: des quartiers calmes."},{fr:"la natation",en:"swimming",note:"Feminine noun; nager is the infinitive to swim."},{fr:"la marche",en:"walking",note:"Feminine noun; marcher is to walk."},{fr:"la danse",en:"dancing / dance",note:"Feminine noun; danser is to dance."},{fr:"une guitare",en:"a guitar",note:"Feminine; plural: des guitares. Jouer de la guitare."},{fr:"un piano",en:"a piano",note:"Masculine; plural: des pianos. Jouer du piano."},{fr:"il y a",en:"there is / there are",note:"The same fixed phrase works for singular and plural."},{fr:"un appartement",en:"a flat / apartment",note:"Masculine; plural: des appartements."},{fr:"une rue calme",en:"a quiet street",note:"Rue is feminine; plural: des rues calmes."},{fr:"le quartier",en:"neighbourhood",note:"Masculine; plural: les quartiers."},{fr:"la plage",en:"beach",note:"Feminine; plural: les plages."}],listenTask:"Track each person’s place of residence, interests and dislikes. Listen for ne… pas. On the sound tracks, compare silent verb endings with liaison in ils aiment."},"u2-family":{minutes:"60–90",objectives:["Describe family relationships and jobs.","Choose possessives by the noun they introduce.","Use masculine/feminine profession forms."],blocks:[{title:"Family relationships",text:"Le père/la mère = father/mother; le frère/la sœur = brother/sister; le fils/la fille = son/daughter. Les parents means parents, not relatives in general. La famille means family. Fille can also mean girl; femme can mean woman or wife, depending on context.",examples:[{fr:"J’ai un frère et une sœur.",en:"I have a brother and a sister.",why:"Use avoir to say which relatives you have."},{fr:"Je suis fille unique.",en:"I’m an only child (woman/girl).",why:"Use fils unique for a man/boy."}]},{title:"Possessives match the owned noun",text:"Choose the possessive using both the owner and the noun: my = mon/ma/mes; familiar your = ton/ta/tes; his/her = son/sa/ses. Mon/ton/son introduce a masculine singular noun; ma/ta/sa a feminine singular noun; mes/tes/ses a plural noun.",examples:[{fr:"son père, sa mère, ses enfants",en:"his/her father, his/her mother, his/her children",why:"Son/sa do not tell you whether the owner is male or female."}]},{title:"Our, polite your and their",text:"Our = notre with a singular noun, nos with a plural. Polite/plural your = votre/vos. Their = leur/leurs. The number of owners does not determine whether the owned noun is plural.",examples:[{fr:"notre fille, nos enfants",en:"our daughter, our children",why:"One daughter uses notre even though there is more than one owner."},{fr:"leur fils, leurs filles",en:"their son, their daughters",why:"The noun’s number chooses leur or leurs."}]},{title:"Before a feminine vowel-initial noun",text:"Use mon/ton/son instead of ma/ta/sa before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel or silent h. The noun remains feminine.",examples:[{fr:"mon amie",en:"my female friend",why:"Amie is feminine, but mon avoids two vowel sounds meeting."}]},{title:"Professions after être",text:"Normally omit the article when simply stating an occupation after être: elle est infirmière. Some profession forms add -e; others change their ending: acteur/actrice, coiffeur/coiffeuse, informaticien/informaticienne, infirmier/infirmière. Fleuriste has the same singular spelling for both genders.",examples:[{fr:"Mon frère est coiffeur. Ma sœur est actrice.",en:"My brother is a hairdresser. My sister is an actress.",why:"Match the profession form to the person."}]},{title:"Relationship status and family questions",text:"Marié/mariée means married; célibataire means single. Un petit ami is a boyfriend, une petite amie a girlfriend. Use combien de + plural noun to ask how many.",examples:[{fr:"Tu as combien de frères et sœurs ?",en:"How many brothers and sisters do you have?",why:"Combien de asks a quantity."},{fr:"Ma sœur est mariée. Elle a deux enfants.",en:"My sister is married. She has two children.",why:"Être describes status; avoir describes having children."}]}],pronunciation:[["Possessive liaison","Link mon ami with /n/ and mes amis with /z/. The final written s is not always pronounced; liaison happens before the following vowel sound."],["Listen for profession endings","Coiffeur/coiffeuse and acteur/actrice change sound. Fleuriste has the same written and spoken form for both genders."]],worked:[{prompt:"Translate “her brother”, then “his sister”.",steps:["Find the owned nouns: frère is masculine; sœur is feminine.","Both owners use the son/sa/ses set.","Choose son for frère and sa for sœur."],answer:"son frère ; sa sœur",english:"her brother; his sister"},{prompt:"Say “Our daughter is a nurse”.",steps:["Daughter is singular: use notre, not nos.","A daughter is feminine: use infirmière.","Do not add an indefinite article after est for a simple occupation."],answer:"Notre fille est infirmière.",english:"Our daughter is a nurse."}],practice:{kind:"checked",instruction:"Write your answer in French. The complete question is shown with each input. Accents, capitalisation and ordinary punctuation do not affect checking.",hint:"Return to Learn or Worked examples if you need a pattern. Try before revealing the answer.",fields:[{prompt:"Complete “my sister”: ___ sœur.",answer:"ma",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Sœur is feminine singular.",why:"Ma agrees with the feminine singular noun sœur."},{prompt:"Complete “my parents”: ___ parents.",answer:"mes",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Parents is plural.",why:"Mes is used with a plural owned noun."},{prompt:"Complete “her brother”: ___ frère.",answer:"son",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The owned noun is masculine.",why:"Son agrees with frère even though the owner is a woman."},{prompt:"Complete “his mother”: ___ mère.",answer:"sa",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The owned noun is feminine.",why:"Sa agrees with mère even though the owner is a man."},{prompt:"Complete “our daughter”: ___ fille.",answer:"notre",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The noun is singular.",why:"Notre is singular; nos would introduce a plural noun."},{prompt:"Complete “my female friend”: ___ amie.",answer:"mon",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Amie begins with a vowel.",why:"Use mon before a feminine vowel-initial noun."},{prompt:"Complete: Elle est ___ . (female nurse)",answer:"infirmière",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"-ier becomes -ière.",why:"Infirmière is the feminine form of infirmier."},{prompt:"Complete: Ma sœur est ___ . (actress)",answer:"actrice",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"The feminine of acteur changes its ending.",why:"Acteur becomes actrice, not acteure in this taught pair."},{prompt:"Complete “their children”: ___ enfants.",answer:"leurs",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Enfants is plural.",why:"Leurs introduces a plural owned noun."},{prompt:"Complete: Il est ___ . (only child, male)",answer:"fils unique",aliases:[],options:[],hint:"Use the masculine family phrase.",why:"Je suis fils unique / il est fils unique describes a male only child."}]},task:{prompt:"Introduce a real or fictional family. Give at least three relationships, two professions and one relationship status. Ask your partner how many siblings they have.",model:"Voici ma famille. Mon père est professeur. Ma mère est infirmière. Ma sœur est actrice et elle est célibataire. Mon frère est marié. Il a deux enfants. Et toi, tu as combien de frères et sœurs ?",english:"This is my family. My father is a teacher. My mother is a nurse. My sister is an actress and she is single. My brother is married. He has two children. And you, how many brothers and sisters do you have?",checks:["I chose possessives using each noun’s gender and number.","I used the correct profession forms.","I used être for jobs/status and avoir for children/siblings.","I can understand and answer a sibling question."]},mistakes:[{wrong:"her brother = sa frère",correct:"her brother = son frère",why:"The gender belongs to frère, not the owner."},{wrong:"nos fille",correct:"notre fille",why:"Fille is singular."},{wrong:"Elle est une infirmière. (simple profession statement)",correct:"Elle est infirmière.",why:"Normally omit the article after être when stating an occupation."}],tracks:[39,41,42,43,44],resources:[{material:"book",page:38,label:"Family relationships"},{material:"book",page:39,label:"Possessives"},{material:"book",page:40,label:"Professions"},{material:"workbook",page:22,label:"Possessives"},{material:"workbook",page:23,label:"Professions"},{material:"workbook",page:24,label:"Family"},{material:"workbook",page:25,label:"Family message"},{material:"workbook",page:26,label:"Unit 2 review"},{material:"workbook",page:28,label:"Unit 2 DELF"}],words:[{fr:"le père / la mère",en:"father / mother",note:"Masculine / feminine; plural: les pères / les mères."},{fr:"le fils / la fille",en:"son / daughter",note:"Masculine / feminine; plural: les fils / les filles."},{fr:"un oncle / une tante",en:"uncle / aunt",note:"Plural: des oncles / des tantes."},{fr:"un cousin / une cousine",en:"male cousin / female cousin",note:"Plural: des cousins / des cousines."},{fr:"un neveu / une nièce",en:"nephew / niece",note:"Plural: des neveux / des nièces."},{fr:"un acteur / une actrice",en:"actor / actress",note:"Plural: des acteurs / des actrices."},{fr:"un informaticien / une informaticienne",en:"IT specialist",note:"Masculine / feminine forms; plurals add -s."},{fr:"un fleuriste / une fleuriste",en:"florist",note:"Same singular word for both genders; plural: des fleuristes."},{fr:"marié / mariée",en:"married",note:"Masculine / feminine adjective; plural adds -s."},{fr:"un petit ami / une petite amie",en:"boyfriend / girlfriend",note:"Plural: des petits amis / des petites amies."},{fr:"mes parents",en:"my parents",note:"Parents is plural: mon père et ma mère. It does not mean relatives in general."},{fr:"mon frère",en:"my brother",note:"Frère is masculine; plural: mes frères."},{fr:"ma sœur",en:"my sister",note:"Sœur is feminine; plural: mes sœurs."},{fr:"notre fille",en:"our daughter",note:"Fille is feminine singular; plural: nos filles."},{fr:"leurs enfants",en:"their children",note:"Enfant can refer to a boy or girl; plural: leurs enfants."}],listenTask:"Make a small family tree while listening. Attach each job to the right relative and distinguish a spouse, sibling and child. Listen for mon/ma/mes and son/sa/ses."}};export{r as f};
